+\subsection{Semantic boundary under real-valued relaxation}
+\label{sec:soft-boundary}
+
+The invariant in Equation~\eqref{eq:invariant} is Boolean. A real-valued interpretation requires a separate contract. For a truth table $C=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, ordinary real contraction gives
+\begin{equation}
+ B_C(p,q)=[p,1-p]C[q,1-q]^T
+ =d+(b-d)p+(c-d)q+(a-b-c+d)pq.
+ \label{eq:soft-contraction}
+\end{equation}
+For independent Bernoulli inputs this is the probability of the represented Boolean event. It is ordinary multilinear interpolation, not XOR--AND matrix multiplication or a new probability calculus. Weighted model counting and semantic loss already provide syntax-independent probability evaluation under declared base-variable models~\cite{Kimmig2017,Xu2018}.
+
+This whole-table interpretation differs from recursively applying soft kernels to subexpressions with shared variables. PALO uses $pq$ for conjunction, $p+q-pq$ for disjunction, $1-p+pq$ for implication, and $1-p-q+2pq$ for primitive equivalence; it explicitly recognizes the independence approximation and the failure of several classical laws~\cite[Sections~3.2, 3.5--3.6]{Stehr2022}. In particular, the Boolean fusion already established in Equation~\eqref{eq:worked-fusion},
+\[
+ (X\Rightarrow\neg Y)\bxor(\neg Y\Rightarrow X)\equiv X\Leftrightarrow Y,
+\]
+does not preserve recursive soft evaluation. Extending XOR by $u+v-2uv$, at $p=q=1/2$ the two implication values are $3/4$, so the source evaluates to $3/8$, whereas the fused equivalence kernel evaluates to $1/2$. The Boolean theorem remains valid; the two numerical interpretations make different claims.
+
+A useful sufficient condition is that each primitive composition uses disjoint base-variable supports, so recursive bilinear evaluation remains multilinear. More generally, for a finite propositional expression $e$ built from these kernels, let $s_e$ be its recursive polynomial after identifying repeated base variables. The whole-table extension agrees with $s_e$ on $[0,1]^n$ exactly when $s_e$ is affine separately in every coordinate, after coefficient cancellation. Indeed, both agree at all Boolean corners, and a multilinear polynomial is uniquely determined by those corners; equality on the full cube conversely gives polynomial identity. This elementary criterion does not imply an efficient unrestricted algorithm: polynomial expansion can grow exponentially. Same-frame metadata alone is insufficient for this additional contract.
+
+Any future numerical backend should distinguish Boolean equivalence, equality of the chosen recursive soft function, event equivalence under a specified joint model, and thresholded-decision equality on a declared domain. Equal event marginals alone do not authorize contextual replacement: independent fair events $X,Y$ both have probability $1/2$, but $\Pr(X\land X)=1/2$ and $\Pr(X\land Y)=1/4$. Mathematical gradient preservation additionally requires the same differentiable parameterization and function equality on its domain; custom backward rules require their own contract. PALO's recursive crisp interpretation also differs from thresholding only the final soft value~\cite[Section~3.7]{Stehr2022}. These distinctions bound possible extensions of this Boolean compiler; no integrated differentiable backend, training improvement, or CM-specific advantage is claimed. Ordinary framed truth tables support the same checks.
